ages/icon_pdf.gif","alt":"Adobe PDF file","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":16,"renderedHeight":16,"naturalWidth":16,"naturalHeight":16,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"GOOD","altReason":"present, no red flags","bytes":6058},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/TemplatePackage/images/icon_pdf.gif","alt":"Adobe PDF file","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":16,"renderedHeight":16,"naturalWidth":16,"naturalHeight":16,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"GOOD","altReason":"present, no red flags","bytes":6058},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/figures/s6005a1f1.gif","alt":"Figure 1 is a bar graph showing annual rates for traumatic brain injury (TBI) deaths, by age group and sex, in the United States during 1997-2007. The TBI-related mortality rate was highest among persons aged ≥75 years, especially those aged ≥85 years. During each year of this reporting period, the TBI-related death rates among males in each age and race group were higher than those among females, especially among persons aged 20-24 years. In this age group, males had rates at least four times higher than those for females.","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":481,"renderedHeight":318,"naturalWidth":481,"naturalHeight":318,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"TOO_LONG","altReason":"alt text is 529 characters — consider a caption or description","bytes":13125},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/figures/s6005a1f2.gif","alt":"Figure 2 is a line graph showing age-adjusted rates for traumatic brain injury (TBI) deaths, by year and race/ethnicity, in the United States during 1990-2007. American Indians/Alaska Natives (AI/ANs) had the highest annual average TBI-related death rates, blacks had the second-highest annual average rates, and Hispanics had the lowest rates. During this reporting period, the annual TBI-related death rates decreased for all racial/ethnic groups, especially for AI/ANs and blacks. ","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":481,"renderedHeight":319,"naturalWidth":481,"naturalHeight":319,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"TOO_LONG","altReason":"alt text is 483 characters — consider a caption or description","bytes":14738},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/figures/s6005a1f3.gif","alt":"Figure 3 is a bar graph showing the percentage of traumatic brain injury (TBI) deaths, by race/ethnicity and external mechanism of injury, in the United States during 1997-2007. Firearm-related events (34.8%), motor vehicle-related events (31.4%), and fall-related events (16.7%) were the leading causes of TBI-related death. Blacks had the highest annual average rate of firearm-related TBI deaths, and whites had the second highest rates. AI/ANs had the highest annual average rate of motor vehicle-related TBI deaths, and whites had the second-highest annual average rate. AI/ANs had the highest annual average rate of fall-related TBI deaths, and whites had the second-highest annual average rate.","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":481,"renderedHeight":366,"naturalWidth":481,"naturalHeight":366,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"TOO_LONG","altReason":"alt text is 701 characters — consider a caption or description","bytes":17476},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/figures/s6005a1f4.gif","alt":"Figure 4 is a line graph showing the age-adjusted rates for traumatic brain injury (TBI) deaths, by year and external mechanism of injury, in the United States during 1990-2007. Rates of fall-related TBI deaths increased by 59.6%, for firearm-related TBI deaths decreased by 13.6%, and for motor vehicle-related TBI deaths decreased by 22.0%. The observed increases and decreases by each external mechanism occurred almost at a constant rate each year during the reporting period.","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":481,"renderedHeight":336,"naturalWidth":481,"naturalHeight":336,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"TOO_LONG","altReason":"alt text is 480 characters — consider a caption or description","bytes":13063},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/figures/s6005a1f5.gif","alt":"Figure 5 is a line graph showing the average annual rates for traumatic brain injury (TBI) deaths, by age group and external mechanism of injury, in the United States during 1997-2007. Firearm-related TBI death rates were highest among persons aged 20-24 and ≥75 years. Motor vehicle-related TBI death rates were highest among persons aged 15-24 years. Fall-related TBI death rates were highest among adults aged ≥75 years and increased significantly with age. In each age group and for each external cause, males had higher rates of TBI-related death than females. ","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":481,"renderedHeight":323,"naturalWidth":481,"naturalHeight":323,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"TOO_LONG","altReason":"alt text is 565 characters — consider a caption or description","bytes":16979},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/TemplatePackage/images/icon_pdf.gif","alt":"Adobe PDF file","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":16,"renderedHeight":16,"naturalWidth":16,"naturalHeight":16,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"GOOD","altReason":"present, no red flags","bytes":6058},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/TemplatePackage/images/icon_word.gif","alt":"Microsoft Word file","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":fa